# acp-vscode — Environment Variables Example
#
# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in your values.
# The ".env" file is in .gitignore and will NEVER be committed.
#
# These variables are referenced in your agent configurations via
# the ${env:VAR_NAME} placeholder syntax in VS Code settings.
# Example settings.json snippet:
#
#   "acp.agents": {
#     "Claude Code": {
#       "command": "npx",
#       "args": ["--yes", "@agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp@latest"],
#       "env": {
#         "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY": "${env:ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}"
#       }
#     }
#   }
#
# The extension resolves ${env:VAR_NAME} at process spawn time from the
# host shell environment — no secrets are stored in VS Code settings.

# ── IBM watsonx Code Assistant (IBM Bob) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Required to connect to IBM Bob via ACP.
# Obtain your key from: https://cloud.ibm.com/iam/apikeys
# Set this in your shell profile (~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc) so VS Code inherits it:
#   export IBM_BOB_API_KEY=your-key-here
# Alternatively set acp.ibmBobApiKey in VS Code settings (marked secret).
IBM_BOB_API_KEY=

# ── IBM watsonx endpoint (optional — defaults to the public watsonx.ai endpoint) ──
IBM_BOB_ENDPOINT=

# ── Anthropic / Claude Code ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── Google Gemini CLI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ── OpenAI / Codex ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── GitHub Copilot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub Copilot uses browser-based OAuth — no key needed here.
# Run: npx @github/copilot-language-server@latest login

# ── Custom agent variables ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MY_AGENT_API_KEY=
# MY_AGENT_BASE_URL=
